Shift-cut hazard predictor: predictions recorded BEFORE any of these models was run
2026-09-09. Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G), resnet_env17, onnx only. NO NPU SESSION was
built by this run and no hardware context was taken; it reads ONNX files and computes.

THIS LOG IS HALF AN EXPERIMENT. It exists to be committed before the hardware runs it sets
up, so that the outcome cannot be read back into the prediction. The result is in
results/quant/shift_cut_forward_results.log.

WHAT IS BEING TESTED, AND WHY IT NEEDED TESTING. quant/shift_cut.py (on branch `main`,
unmerged at the time of writing) formulates a feasibility bound for the DPU's requantizer:
the 32-bit accumulator is mapped to int8 through a 15-bit multiplier M and an arithmetic
right shift sigma constrained to [0, 31], so a scale triple whose ideal factor
A = (S_x * S_w) / S_y cannot be written as M * 2^-sigma in that window is infeasible on the
hardware. The audit flags such convolutions. Its evidence so far is entirely RETRODICTIVE:
every model it had been pointed at already had a known outcome. That makes it a pattern that
fits the past, not yet a predictor.

The failure class matters because this repo has hit it: a model that is correct under CPU
execution and wrong on the DPU, invisible to placement reports. BiSeNetV2 is the example --
402/404 nodes placed, 13.12 ms, beating the iGPU, and 15.33% pixel accuracy on physical
hardware against 59.47% under CPU simulation.

METHOD. Every XINT8 artifact in models/ was cross-referenced against every .log and .md in
results/ and docs/, and those with no NPU mention anywhere near them were taken as the
candidate set. The audit was then run over ALL of them and EVERY verdict is printed below.
The set was fixed before any result was seen and nothing has been dropped from it.

