THE TWO THAT WILL BE RUN, AND WHY THOSE TWO.

  sesr_m7_fp32_xint8, predicted to fail on every one of its nine operations. This is the
  boldest claim available: 100% of the model is called infeasible. SESR-M7 as an
  ARCHITECTURE is one of this repo's successes -- 1.48 ms on the NPU, a 3.02x win over the
  integrated GPU, and measured super-resolution quality on hardware of 34.06 dB PSNR on
  Set5 against a 35.64 dB float reference. If this artifact runs correctly, a 9-of-9
  prediction is a false positive of the worst kind.

  realesrgan_compact_r64_xint8, predicted clean, 0 of 34. It tests the other direction on a
  different architecture, and it has no NPU record of any kind.

A NOTE THAT ALREADY WEIGHS AGAINST THE PREDICTOR, RECORDED HERE RATHER THAN AFTER THE FACT.
The audit's own committed log (results/quant/shift_cut_feasibility.log, branch `main`) shows
`sesr_m7_xint8.onnx` -- a sibling artifact of the same architecture, and one of the six the
audit's author ran -- at 9 of 9 violations, 100%. That model's measured NPU quality is the
34.06 dB quoted above. The author's commit message lists ResNet-50, YOLOv8n, YOLOv8n-pose and
FastDepth as sitting cleanly and does not mention this result. So a false positive may
already be present in the audit's own evidence, and the runs set up here are partly a check
of that.

WHAT WOULD COUNT AS WHAT
  Prediction confirmed  a flagged model's NPU output departs structurally from its own CPU
                        output -- large deviation relative to peak AND a fallen correlation
                        -- while a clean model's does not.
  Prediction refuted    a flagged model's NPU output tracks its CPU output at ordinary
                        requantization noise, correlation essentially 1.
  Inconclusive          the EP does not place the model, so the comparison says nothing
                        about the DPU. Placement is reported for exactly this reason.
